**Read:** [The E factor 25 years on — Roger A. Sheldon](../sources/E-Factor-Sheldon.lingua#r_37VYJ12P4GGC89VRW44RMBK0AT), the whole perspective, definitions and complete worked route examples; [Química verde no Brasil: 2010–2030 — MCT/CGEE](../sources/Quimica-Verde-no-Brasil-CGEE.lingua#r_6C2MG2T0DTT5FDCA24NFY2FTW5), the full relevant process/strategy and resource chapters; [Biochemical conversion of corn stover to ethanol — Humbird and colleagues](../sources/Biochemical-Ethanol-Design-Humbird.lingua#r_123G817BYJFAVYGA0ZVK3J5FJH), the complete material/energy, co-product, economic and sensitivity basis.

Atom economy describes a stoichiometric allocation of reactant atoms to a chosen product; conversion, selectivity, isolated yield and accepted output describe other aspects of the process. Process mass intensity (PMI) divides defined material inputs by useful product, while an E factor divides defined nonproduct or waste output by product. State the inclusion rules and boundary before comparing numbers.

For an original closed steady account with 500 kg crossing as material input, 100 kg of chosen product and 400 kg of counted nonproduct output, PMI=5 and E=4. Internal recycle does not cross that boundary again. The relation PMI=1+E holds only with compatible accounting conventions, no omitted accumulation and all remaining output included in the nonproduct definition.

Mass metrics do not alone determine hazard, water quality, energy, carbon, resource availability, economic viability or actual function. A co-product credit or excluded solvent/water stream can change a comparison without changing the physical process. Keep life-cycle and allocation assumptions explicit and test them against alternatives.

**Work:** reproduce a complete primary route comparison, independently calculating its main metrics and checking every inclusion rule. Extend it with a consequential energy, water, hazard or lifetime question and a changed boundary. Explain which ranking is supported, which depends on an assumption and what new evidence could reverse the conclusion.

**Continue:** [Interpret chemical-process economics through the actual technical system and dated assumptions](../operation/Process-economics-scale-quality-and-resource-assumptions.lingua#r_67JCR48DW88R29ED6RMGBMPDHN).

